      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e what is this used for? what if TK_ABSENT is used, does the min still apply?\n\nThe min is used to ensure that the top row keys contain at least 10 keys. Anything less than that is not allowed (Coreboot checks this). If one of those 10 keys is ommitted by EC by saying TK_ABSENT, that is still an error but less severe because atleast the other keys will continue to function. \n\n\u003e \n\u003e Also, it is work adding a note that value for MAX cannot change will creating a new version of the response struct.\n\nWill do.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e Another way to structure the response would be to use the num_top_row_keys count as the size of the a uint8_t action_keys[0] as the last member of the response. Then we don\u0027t have to send extra bytes for keys we aren\u0027t using\n\nI\u0027m not sure if I follow the suggestion. Can you elaborate? Are you suggesting to include the array size as the first element of the array? I think 5 bytes is worth the clarity the current scheme brings. WDYT?",
